# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the sanitizer CI job (.github/workflows/sanitizers.yml).
#
# These cover third-party driver/runtime allocations that legitimately persist to
# process exit (TLS caches, JIT contexts, dlopen'd ICD globals) — not bugs we can
# fix. Each entry is scoped to a library/symbol substring so FIRST-PARTY leaks are
# still reported: never add a `vg::` / `volumetric_kit::` frame here. If a real
# first-party leak appears, fix it — do not suppress it.
#
# IMPORTANT — scope entries to the *allocating* code, never to a module every
# call transits. LSan's GetSuppressionForStack walks the whole allocation stack
# and returns on the first match, so an entry naming a pass-through module
# absorbs first-party leaks that merely called through it. `leak:libvulkan.so`
# used to sit below and did exactly that: the loader trampoline is in the stack
# of EVERY Vulkan call, so a VkImage the RetireQueue failed to destroy, a
# VkPipeline leaked on an error path, or a leaked VMA allocation were all
# silently swallowed — neutering the job CLAUDE.md relies on for the
# leak/double-free class. Prefer function-name substrings (`loader_…`, `lvp_…`)
# that only the third-party allocator itself can match.

# Mesa / lavapipe — the software Vulkan ICD used on the GPU-less CI runner.
leak:libvulkan_lvp
leak:lvp_
leak:libgallium
leak:swrast
leak:util_queue
leak:libLLVM

# Vulkan loader: ICD/layer discovery state that persists to exit. Scoped to the
# loader's own `loader_*` allocation helpers — a function-name substring no
# first-party frame can match — and deliberately NOT to the libvulkan.so module
# (see the note above).
leak:loader_

# Khronos validation layer (enabled via the Debug build) internal state.
leak:libVkLayer_khronos_validation

# dlopen-resident globals from loading the driver / layers.
leak:__libc_dlopen
